imidazole-4-acetaldehyde from 0.03 M erythrose and 0.03 M formamidine at about pH 6 and 80 C, with aldehyde yields up to about 1.6 percent relative to erythrose. The main caveat is environmental availability and stability of both erythrose and especially formamidine (Shen et al., 1987; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Erythrose-formamidine condensation to imidazole-4-glycol", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH about 6; about 12 h; no added mineral catalyst; warm concentrated aqueous mixture"}, "mechanism": "The same imidazole-forming condensation network that gives the aldehyde can also be trapped as the more hydrated glycol reservoir.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. reported imidazole-4-glycol as a major coproduct in the erythrose plus formamidine system, with yields up to about 6.8 percent relative to erythrose. Including it is important because it provides a chemically reasonable reservoir for later regeneration of the imidazole aldehyde, although its full environmental progression to histidine is partly inferential (Shen et al., 1987)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Collapse of imidazole-4-glycol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly acidic to near-neutral aqueous cycling; dehydration or gem-diol collapse"}, "mechanism": "Reversible loss of water from the hydrated side-chain carbonyl regenerates the free aldehyde.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. discussed imidazole-4-glycol as a plausible precursor or reservoir for imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, especially under acid-promoted dehydration. The transformation is chemically conservative for a hydrated aldehyde but was not isolated as a separate prebiotic step, so this node is mildly speculative (Shen et al., 1987; Shen et al., 1990)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Erythrose-formaldehyde-ammonia route to imidazole-4-glycol", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH about 6.6; about 12 h; NH4+ in the laboratory system can be represented here by NH3 or NH4+ as reduced nitrogen input"}, "mechanism": "Multicomponent condensation of erythrose, a C1 carbonyl source, and reduced nitrogen forms the imidazole ring while furnishing the side-chain glycol state.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. experimentally observed imidazole-4-glycol in the erythrose plus formaldehyde plus ammonium branch, at about 1.8 percent relative to erythrose. This gives a legitimate alternative entry to the same imidazole-side-chain reservoir, but the route was not experimentally completed to histidine and the required oxidizing equivalent remained unresolved, so it is weaker than the formamidine branch (Shen et al., 1987)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Strecker formation of histidine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "near-neutral to mildly basic aqueous stage after aldehyde formation"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium with imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and cyanide adds to that electrophile to give the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "The imidazole aldehyde to aminonitrile conversion is the conventional mechanistic core of the Shen histidine synthesis. Shen et al. closed the overall aldehyde plus NH3 plus HCN sequence experimentally even though the aminonitrile itself was not the analytically emphasized product, so this step is mechanistically strong but partly inferred as a discrete node (Shen et al., 1990)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "warm aqueous aging or geothermal cycling; slower than the cyanide addition stage"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis converts the alpha-aminonitrile into the amino acid carboxylate.", "reasoning": "This is the terminal hydrolysis step of the Strecker sequence leading to histidine. Shen et al. detected histidine after adding HCN and ammonia to the imidazole-forming mixture, so the aminonitrile-to-acid conversion is required even though it was not separately quantified; under prebiotic conditions it is plausible but kinetically nontrivial, making it a realistic downstream bottleneck (Shen et al., 1990)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "One-pot erythrose-formamidine-Strecker synthesis of histidine", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353K then 300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "warm concentrated aqueous pool; the imidazole aldehyde stage need not be isolated before HCN and NH3 addition"}, "mechanism": "Telescoped sequence in which erythrose and formamidine generate the imidazole aldehyde in situ, which then undergoes Strecker addition and nitrile hydrolysis to histidine.", "reasoning": "This is the strongest targeted prebiotic histidine synthesis reported so far: Shen et al. obtained histidine at about 3.5 percent yield relative to erythrose and characterized it by multiple chromatographic and mass-spectrometric methods. The chemistry itself is strong, but the precursor inventory remains contested because erythrose is hard to enrich and formamidine hydrolyzes readily (Shen et al., 1990; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Electrical-discharge synthesis in CH4-NH3-H2-H2O", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007", "mol_012", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-355K", "pressure": "near 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "water reservoir near 80 C with continuous spark discharge for about 7 days; reduced atmosphere analog"}, "mechanism": "Electrical discharge generates HCN, carbonyls, radicals, and diverse nitrogenous organics that subsequently converge to histidine through unresolved multistep chemistry.", "reasoning": "Jiang et al. reported that a modern Miller-Urey setup produced all non-sulfur protein amino acids except glutamate after seven days, which necessarily includes histidine. This is robust endpoint evidence but not a resolved histidine-specific pathway, so the edge is intentionally coarse-grained (Jiang et al., 2014)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Electrical-discharge synthesis in CH4-NH3-N2-H2O", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "about 200 mbar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "about 30 kV discharge; about 14 day experiment with about 7 days energized exposure; buffered pH about 8.7 or unbuffered pH about 11.1"}, "mechanism": "Spark-driven radical and ion chemistry in methane-ammonia-nitrogen atmospheres produces complex organics that yield histidine through unresolved aqueous and gas-phase sequences.", "reasoning": "Criado-Reyes et al. reported histidine in every reactor configuration, including Teflon systems without borosilicate, with about 0.08 to 0.31 mg histidine per experiment depending on conditions. However the mechanism remains unresolved and histidine identification was less structurally secure than in the Shen route, relying on derivatized GC-MS and library matching rather than a targeted multitechnique proof (Criado-Reyes et al., 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Fischer-Tropsch-type surface synthesis from CO-H2-NH3", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_012", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni", "Al2O3", "clay minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "473-973K", "pressure": "low to moderate", "catalyst": "Ni, alumina, or clay", "aqueous": false, "other": "hot gas-solid catalytic surface chemistry; represented as a net empirical source term rather than an elementary transformation"}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated CO reduction, C-C growth, nitrogen incorporation, and heterocycle plus amino-acid formation over catalytic solids.", "reasoning": "Yoshino et al. reported histidine from CO plus H2 plus NH3 over nickel, alumina, and clay catalysts, and Hayatsu et al. strengthened the claim with deuterated feedstocks and mass spectrometry. Because no histidine-specific intermediate sequence was identified and overall amino-acid yields were low, this reaction is kept as a coarse-grained catalytic family rather than an elementary step (Yoshino et al., 1971; Hayatsu et al., 1971)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "UV/EUV PAH-ice synthesis and hydrolytic release of histidine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "15K irradiation then warming after delivery", "pressure": "below 5e-10 torr during irradiation", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "1:1:1 naphthalene:H2O:NH3 ice irradiated with 4-20 eV photons; laboratory detection required strong-acid hydrolysis of the residue"}, "mechanism": "UV or EUV irradiation of aromatic-water-ammonia ice forms radical-rich refractory products that yield free histidine or histidine-labile precursors upon later hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Chen et al. detected histidine after UV/EUV irradiation of a naphthalene:H2O:NH3 ice and subsequent acid hydrolysis. This is genuine endpoint evidence for an exogenous source term, but the quantum yield is very low, the ice composition is unrealistically NH3-rich, and the hydrolysis step means the experiment cannot distinguish free histidine from acid-labile precursors already embedded in the residue (Chen et al., 2008)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct Shen aldehyde route", "description": "Mechanistically explicit route in which erythrose and formamidine first give imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, then classical Strecker chemistry and nitrile hydrolysis furnish histidine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Shen glycol-reservoir route", "description": "Variant of the explicit Shen chemistry in which the erythrose-formamidine condensation is captured as imidazole-4-glycol before regeneration of the aldehyde and Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Erythrose-formaldehyde-ammonia glycol route", "description": "Alternative Shen 1987 branch that reaches the same imidazole-glycol reservoir without formamidine in the initial condensation step, then proceeds through aldehyde, aminonitrile, and histidine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Formose-assisted direct Shen route", "description": "Adds a speculative formaldehyde-to-erythrose feeder to the direct Shen aldehyde route, connecting simpler C1 feedstock chemistry to the explicit histidine sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_002", "mol_004", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Formose-assisted Shen glycol route", "description": "Adds 