rm or aggregate multistep process, not as an elementary reaction.\",\n        \"Annotate the energy source explicitly as electrical discharge and the medium as a reduced gas phase over water.\",\n        \"Do not imply erythrose, formamidine, or imidazole-aldehyde intermediates unless experimentally shown.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.28\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.31,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.53,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.35,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.62,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n      \"feedback\": \"This route is supported as a quantified endpoint report in modern CH4/NH3/N2/H2O spark-discharge work, but still only as endpoint chemistry. rxn_010 is grossly unbalanced as written, the environment is not a generic surface, and the molecular path to histidine remains unresolved.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Recast rxn_010 as a coarse empirical source term with correct aggregate stoichiometry and discharge conditions.\",\n        \"Use a gas discharge plus aqueous trap environment label rather than Surface.\",\n        \"Keep confidence moderate because the literature reports histidine endpoints but not a resolved histidine-specific mechanism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.31\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.34,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.49,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.43,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.58,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.44,\n      \"feedback\": \"Including the Fischer-Tropsch-type family is literature-consistent, but only as a historical endpoint-producing route. The current reaction omits the essential catalytic context from the laboratory reports, is grossly unbalanced, and does not identify target-specific intermediates.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_011 as an aggregate catalytic source term with explicit catalyst and temperature annotations.\",\n        \"Add sufficient CO, H2, and NH3 stoichiometry and explicit byproducts if it is kept as a reaction node.\",\n        \"Avoid implying a resolved molecular mechanism to histidine from the 1971 experiments.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.20\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.24,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.39,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.32,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.46,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.32,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the weakest pathway representation. The Chen et al. experiment supports histidine only after UV or EUV processing of a 15 K naphthalene/H2O/NH3 ice followed by strong-acid hydrolysis; it does not securely show free histidine as a direct single-step product. rxn_012 is also unbalanced and the Surface label misses the astrophysical ice and vacuum context.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Represent the product as histidine after hydrolysis of irradiated residue, or add an explicit precursor residue node before hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Specify the 15 K PAH-ice, UV or EUV irradiation, and exogenous delivery context rather than a generic surface environment.\",\n        \"If kept as a reaction node, provide aggregate stoichiometry and do not overstate the certainty of free histidine formation.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.63,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The strategy is literature-aware and mostly well ranked: it correctly centers the Shen erythrose + formamidine -> imidazole-4-acetaldehyde -> Strecker chemistry as the only mechanistically explicit histidine route, treats spark, Fischer-Tropsch-type, and PAH-ice chemistry as endpoint-level source terms, and does not force a hydrothermal vent branch that lacks convincing target-specific support. The graph is connected and molecule IDs are used consistently. The main failure is formal network validity: the deterministic balance check gives 0 balanced as written, 1 balanceable with coefficients (rxn_001), and 11 UNBALANCED, so the current network is not a valid stoichiometric reaction network. Key fixes are: rxn_001 should be written as 4 CH2O -> erythrose; rxn_002 needs H2O as product (3 equiv); rxn_003 needs H2O as product (2 equiv); rxn_004 needs H2O as product; rxn_006 needs H2O as product; rxn_007 should be histidine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3; rxn_008 should be rewritten as a balanced net step closer to erythrose + formamidine + HCN -> histidine + 2 H2O, with NH3 treated as reagent or catalytic participant rather than a net reactant; rxn_005 is not valid as written and needs explicit redox and coproduct accounting, especially because the literature branch also gives imidazole-4-ethanol and used ammonium conditions; rxn_009-rxn_012 should be encoded as aggregate empirical multistep source terms with proper stoichiometry, catalysts or energy sources, and realistic environment labels rather than as single reactions. Environmental specification is also too coarse: Biochemical should be warm aqueous prebiotic conditions, electrical discharge is not a surface process, Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry needs explicit catalytic surfaces and high laboratory temperatures, and the PAH route should be a 15 K irradiated ice plus post-irradiation hydrolysis and delivery scenario. So the network has good literature alignment and a credible core concept, but significant stoichiometric and environmental-definition problems keep overall confidence in the moderate range.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluation