 chemistry at about pH 6.6 and 80 C. This branch is experimentally observed, but it is not by itself a completed histidine synthesis; it is included as an alternative precursor reservoir rather than as a strongly validated route to the target (Shen et al., 1987)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Mineral-assisted dehydrogenation of imidazole-4-ethanol", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fe-Ni mineral surface", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-390 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Fe-Ni or magnetite surface", "aqueous": false, "other": "Hot mineral films or low-water interfaces; balanced as alcohol dehydrogenation C5H8N2O -> C5H6N2O + H2."}, "mechanism": "Mineral-assisted alcohol dehydrogenation/oxidation to the imidazole aldehyde.", "reasoning": "This oxidation/dehydrogenation is not demonstrated in the supplied histidine papers and should be treated as speculative. It is included only because Shen et al. (1987) identified imidazole-4-ethanol as a real coproduct and noted that conversion to the aldehyde would be needed for closure, while Vazquez-Salazar et al. (2018) highlighted that the redox step remains unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Strecker addition on imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN- and NH3-bearing aqueous solution; balanced net is imidazole-4-acetaldehyde + NH3 + HCN -> histidine aminonitrile + H2O."}, "mechanism": "Imine/iminium formation followed by cyanide capture to give the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional Strecker capture of the validated imidazole aldehyde. Shen et al. (1990) experimentally closed the route by adding HCN and ammonia to the imidazole-precursor mixture, and the corresponding alpha-aminonitrile is the standard mechanistic intermediate for that chemistry (Shen et al., 1990)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Prolonged aqueous aging or geothermal cycling; balanced net consumes 2 H2O per aminonitrile and liberates NH3."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydrolysis converts the alpha-aminonitrile into the amino acid carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of the alpha-aminonitrile to histidine is the conservative interpretation of the second half of the Shen synthesis. Histidine itself was identified by multiple chromatographic and mass-spectrometric methods in Shen et al. (1990), making this the most mechanistically secure completion step."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "One-pot Shen-Oro histidine synthesis net step", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-355 K then aqueous aging", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Balanced net edge: erythrose + formamidine + HCN -> histidine + 2 H2O. NH3 is omitted because it is consumed in the Strecker stage but regenerated during aminonitrile hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Aggregate two-stage sequence combining imidazole construction with Strecker closure and hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "This is a balanced aggregate representation of the experimentally closed Shen-Oro sequence, not an elementary step: erythrose + formamidine was first heated to generate imidazole intermediates, then HCN/ammonia was added to obtain histidine in 3.5% yield relative to erythrose (Shen et al., 1990). It is included because the paper demonstrated histidine formation without isolating the aldehyde intermediate."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Electrical-discharge histidine source term", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K bulk liquid; plasma microzones much hotter", "pressure": "0.2-1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Surface label used as an atmospheric discharge over water proxy. Net balance shown as 6 CH4 + 3 NH3 + 2 H2O -> histidine + 14 H2; H2 or N2 present in some experiments are treated as atmosphere components rather than required net inputs."}, "mechanism": "Aggregate endpoint-only discharge chemistry involving gas-phase radicals, HCN/carbonyl generation, and secondary aqueous reactions.", "reasoning": "Histidine was reported from reduced-gas discharge chemistry, with Jiang et al. (2014) stating that all non-sulfur protein amino acids except glutamate were formed after seven days, and Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) later reported quantified histidine from CH4/NH3/N2/H2O discharges. Because neither study established a histidine-specific intermediate sequence and the 2021 assignment is less secure than the Shen route, this balanced edge is encoded only as an empirical source term (Jiang et al., 2014; Criado-Reyes et al., 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Fischer-Tropsch-type catalytic histidine source term", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_013", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni", "Al2O3", "clay"], "conditions": {"temperature": "470-970 K", "pressure": "low to moderate", "catalyst": "Ni, Al2O3, or clay", "aqueous": false, "other": "Hydrothermal label here denotes hot catalytic mineral-surface chemistry rather than a demonstrated vent-wall sequence. Balanced net is 6 CO + 4 H2 + 3 NH3 -> histidine + 4 H2O."}, "mechanism": "Aggregate high-temperature heterogeneous surface chemistry involving reduction, C-C bond formation, and nitrogen incorporation.", "reasoning": "Yoshino et al. (1971) reported histidine among amino acids formed from CO/H2/NH3 over Ni, alumina, or clay, and Hayatsu et al. (1971) reinforced the claim with deuterated feedstocks and mass spectrometry to address contamination. The mechanism and histidine-specific yield remained unresolved, and the high laboratory temperatures are not direct proof of a vent-safe accumulation route, so this is kept as a coarse-grained catalytic source term (Yoshino et al., 1971; Hayatsu et al., 1971; Ito et al., 2006)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "UV-EUV PAH-ice histidine source term", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_014", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "15 K irradiation, then warming and hydrolysis", "pressure": "<5e-10 torr during irradiation", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Surface label is a schema proxy for exogenous irradiated ice delivered to a planetary surface. Based on a 1:1:1 naphthalene:H2O:NH3 ice; CO and H2 are bookkeeping coproducts included to balance the aggregate source term."}, "mechanism": "Aggregate photochemical radical chemistry in PAH-rich ice followed by release of histidine or histidine-yielding precursors during hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Chen et al. (2008) detected histidine after UV/EUV irradiation of a 1:1:1 naphthalene:H2O:NH3 ice followed by strong-acid hydrolysis. Because the ice ratios were deliberately NH3- and PAH-rich, the quantum yield was very low, and hydrolysis may have released histidine from acid-labile precursors rather than from free histidine formed in the ice, this edge is encoded as an aggregate photochemical/hydrolytic source term rather than an elementary reaction (Chen et al., 2008)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Full Shen aldehyde route from formaldehyde-derived erythrose", "description": "Speculative formaldehyde-to-erythrose feeding into the experimentally validated erythrose plus formamidine aldehyde branch, followed by Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Late-stage Shen aldehyde route from accumulated erythrose", "description": "Starts from pre-concentrated erythrose and uses the best-supported precursor step to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde before Strecker conversion to histidine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Full glycol-reservoir route from formaldehyde-derived erythrose", "description": "A longer branch in which erythrose and formamidine first give the observed coproduct imidazole-4-glycol, which then collapses to the validated aldehyde precursor before Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_008", "mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Erythrose-to-histidine via the glycol reservoir", "description": "Begins after erythrose has accumulated and follows the experimentally observed imidazole-4-glycol branch into the semi-speculative dehydration step and then the validated Strecker sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Formaldehyde-ammonia alcohol branch", "description": "Uses the observed erythrose plus formaldehyde plus ammonium branch to imidazole-4-ethanol, then a speculative mineral-assisted oxidation to the aldehyde, followed by Strecker closure.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_009", "mol_007", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Direct Strecker completion from imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "description": "Late-stage closure from the validated imidazole aldehyde precursor through histidine aminonitrile to histidine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_0