   \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous pool, pH about 6, ca. 12 h heating; coproduct branch of the same imidazole-forming system.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same erythrose-formamidine condensation network can terminate as a hydrated glycol/diol reservoir instead of the free aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol was directly observed as a substantial coproduct in the erythrose plus formamidine system of Shen et al. (1987). It is therefore justified as a real network node, although it is less directly tied to histidine than the aldehyde branch. Its significance is as a plausible aldehyde reservoir rather than as a complete histidine route by itself (Shen et al., 1987).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydrative collapse of imidazole-4-glycol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly acidic aqueous episodes or drying concentration favor loss of water from the glycol/hydrate reservoir.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted dehydration of the glycol or hydrated aldehyde regenerates the corresponding imidazole acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This branch is chemically plausible and consistent with Shen et al.'s discussion of imidazole-4-glycol as a precursor reservoir, but it was not closed experimentally with the same strength as direct aldehyde formation. It is therefore included as a partially supported conversion rather than a fully demonstrated standalone route (Shen et al., 1987; Shen et al., 1990).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker formation of histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN and NH3 added to the warm imidazole-aldehyde-containing solution; conventional Strecker conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine or iminium formation from the aldehyde and ammonia is followed by cyanide addition to give the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Once imidazole-4-acetaldehyde exists, the literature-supported route to histidine is a standard Strecker sequence. Shen et al. (1990) added HCN and ammonia directly to the aldehyde-generating mixture and identified histidine downstream; the mechanistically crucial immediate precursor is the corresponding aminonitrile. This is the clearest target-specific step after imidazole-aldehyde formation (Shen et al., 1990).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine aminonitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged aqueous aging, wet-dry rehydration, or geothermal cycling; hydrolysis slower than cyanide addition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis converts the aminonitrile into the amino acid while releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Representing aminonitrile hydrolysis explicitly clarifies the mass balance of the Shen histidine synthesis. Shen et al. (1990) identified histidine after adding HCN and NH3 to the aldehyde-forming mixture; splitting the chemistry into aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis reflects standard Strecker logic and avoids treating NH3 as a net consumptive reagent. This is chemically strong, though slower than the preceding Strecker addition step (Shen et al., 1990).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Shen-Or\u00f3 histidine synthesis (aggregate net step)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm mildly acidic aqueous stage; balanced net representation of the successful one-pot sequence. NH3 was present experimentally but cancels in the net stoichiometry because it is regenerated during nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aggregate of imidazole-ring construction from erythrose and formamidine, Strecker aminonitrile formation, and final hydrolysis to histidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This node summarizes the experimentally successful one-pot aqueous route closed by Shen et al. (1990). It is intentionally encoded as a balanced net transformation rather than an elementary reaction. Experimental support is strong for histidine formation itself, but geochemical criticism remains focused on whether erythrose and formamidine could coexist at useful concentrations in realistic settings (Shen et al., 1990; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Electrical-discharge reduced-gas source term for histidine (aggregate)\",\